menu "CSI from a router configuration"

    config CSI_ROUTER_SSID
        string "Access point SSID"
        default "myssid"

    config CSI_ROUTER_PASSWORD
        string "Access point password"
        default "mypassword"
        help
            Leave empty for an open network.

    choice CSI_ROUTER_BAND
        prompt "Band to use"
        default CSI_ROUTER_BAND_AUTO
        help
            The C5 can associate on either band. Pinning it is the only way to
            be sure which one you measured when the AP advertises the same SSID
            on both - and measuring a 5 GHz link is the reason to use a C5 here
            rather than any other Espressif part.

        config CSI_ROUTER_BAND_AUTO
            bool "Either (WIFI_BAND_MODE_AUTO)"
        config CSI_ROUTER_BAND_5G_ONLY
            bool "5 GHz only"
        config CSI_ROUTER_BAND_2G_ONLY
            bool "2.4 GHz only"
    endchoice

    config CSI_ROUTER_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Milliseconds between pings"
        range 5 5000
        default 20
        help
            Sets the CSI rate: roughly one echo reply, and so one CSI record,
            per ping. 20 ms gives about 50 records per second.

            esp_ping schedules with vTaskDelayUntil() at this interval, so with
            the project's CONFIG_FREERTOS_HZ=1000 anything down to a few
            milliseconds is honoured. The real floor is the round-trip time: if
            the gateway takes longer than the interval to answer, the rate drops
            to whatever the link sustains.

    config CSI_ROUTER_PRINT_CSV
        bool "Print one CSV line per record"
        default y

    config CSI_ROUTER_FORCE_LLTF
        bool "Report L-LTF instead of HT/VHT/HE-LTF"
        default n
        help
            Say y to see the other buffer format. On the C5 this does two
            things, not one:

              n - HT/VHT/HE-LTF, 8-bit int8 pairs, two bytes per tone. Record
                  length follows whatever PHY the AP's rate control picked:
                  490 for HE20, 234 for HT40, 114 for HT20.
              y - L-LTF only, always 106 bytes - but in 12-bit packing, which is
                  FOUR bytes per tone and yields 26 tones, not 53.

            That second effect is undocumented: ESP-IDF's CSI page still says
            two bytes per sub-carrier for every case on this chip. The shared
            component decodes both and labels each record in the "packing" CSV
            column, so it is safe to toggle here - but any external tool you
            feed the CSV to has to respect that column.

    config CSI_ROUTER_VAL_SCALE
        int "CSI value scaling (val_scale_cfg)"
        range 0 8
        default 0
        help
            Scales the reported I/Q values. ESP-IDF documents only the range,
            0-8 on the C5. Leave it at 0 unless the printed values clip at
            +/-127 or sit in the noise.

endmenu
